# PART 2 — UNRESOLVED DECISIONS REGISTER

## 5. Do not invent these decisions

Create a visible requirements register and track each item using these statuses:

- Confirmed by the specification.
- Proposed technical decision.
- Requires business confirmation.
- Blocked by an external dependency.
- Implemented and verified.

The following questions must be resolved before their affected production workflows are activated.

### 5.1 Customer account assignment

The specified account-number format begins at `BRB-C-ID131` and continues sequentially.

However, the source does not establish whether every new customer should receive an account number automatically or whether customers must already have an account number assigned by BRB.

The registration process requires customers to enter their BRB account number.

Do not invent a registration policy that contradicts this process.

Propose a secure workflow in which BRB can pre-register eligible customers or securely assign their account numbers. Ask for confirmation of how initial customer records are created and how the business verifies ownership of an account number and mobile number.

### 5.2 Technology and hosting

Select a suitable full-stack architecture compatible with the actual environment.

If Google AI Studio supports only part of the required infrastructure, identify which external services are necessary.

Do not assume that Google AI Studio itself provides a complete production database, unrestricted backend hosting, SMS gateway, file storage or scheduled backup service.

### 5.3 OTP and SMS provider

The business requires mobile OTP verification and SMS notifications.

The actual provider, credentials, sender ID, pricing, supported Pakistani mobile networks, message templates and delivery-report capabilities are not specified.

Implement an integration interface and configuration requirements. Do not invent credentials or report successful delivery without provider confirmation.

### 5.4 Administrative roles

The specification requires staff/admin accounts and a main administrator, but it does not fully define individual permissions.

Propose a least-privilege permission matrix. Obtain confirmation before granting sensitive financial, user-management or backup-restoration permissions.

### 5.5 Accounting policies

The specification defines invoice debits and payment credits but does not fully define:

- Opening balances and migration of existing customer accounts.
- Overpayments and negative outstanding balances.
- Payment allocation when multiple invoices exist.
- Partial payments and invoice settlement.
- Invoice correction and cancellation after posting.
- Payment reversal and refund procedures.
- Whether posted transactions can be edited directly or must be corrected through adjustment entries.
- Currency precision, rounding and tax treatment.

Do not invent tax rules, payment allocation policies or credit policies.

Recommend safe accounting controls and identify which policies need approval.

### 5.6 Order and invoice relationship

The system must support invoicing an approved order and creating invoices independently for offline sales.

The following remain unresolved:

- Whether one order can produce multiple invoices.
- Whether one invoice can combine multiple orders.
- How partially delivered quantities affect invoices.
- Whether a confirmed order may be revised and how revisions are recorded.
- How an invoice relates to cancelled or incomplete orders.

Design the data model to avoid unnecessary restrictions, but do not enable conflicting business workflows before their rules are confirmed.

### 5.7 Currency, timezone and dates

The source uses `Rs.` in its examples and Urdu messages.

Confirm the formal currency, timezone, invoice date policy and accounting period rules. Use configurable settings where appropriate.

Do not hardcode assumptions about tax rates or fiscal-year boundaries.

### 5.8 Deployment and operations

Confirm the production domain, database project, storage provider, backup destination, deployment owner and operational access requirements.

Do not deploy to a public production environment or use real customer data without appropriate authorization and configuration.

