# PART 16 — DATABASE AND DATA MODEL

## 50. Design the database before building dependent features

Produce a documented schema and relationship diagram before implementing all business workflows.

Choose the database based on the actual environment and requirements. A relational database is a strong candidate because the application requires financial transactions, referential integrity, sequential identifiers, reporting and consistent ledger updates.

Do not assume that a particular database has already been configured.

Use migrations or an equivalent reproducible schema-management approach.

## 51. Required logical entities

Design an appropriate data model covering at least these logical entities. These are required concepts, not a demand that every concept must map to a separate physical table.

### Authentication and administration

- Authentication identities.
- Customer profiles.
- Staff/admin profiles.
- Roles and permissions.
- Sessions or authentication-related records where appropriate.

### Products

- Products.
- Product categories.
- Product image references.
- Product activation state.

### Orders

- Orders.
- Immutable original requested items.
- Approved order items.
- Administrator-added items.
- Quantity-reduction reasons.
- Order status history.

### Invoices

- Invoices.
- Invoice line items.
- Invoice numbering sequence.
- Invoice correction or revision history.
- Invoice-to-order relationships where applicable.

### Payments and accounting

- Payments.
- Payment correction or reversal records where approved.
- Ledger entries.
- Opening balances where approved.
- Running/current balance support.
- Accounting audit records.

### Notifications

- Notifications.
- Notification recipients.
- Delivery attempts and statuses.
- Provider references or callbacks where supported.

### System operations

- Application settings.
- Audit logs.
- Backup metadata or operational records where applicable.

## 52. Data integrity

Enforce:

- Unique customer account numbers.
- Unique invoice numbers.
- Unique order numbers.
- Foreign-key or equivalent relationship integrity.
- Valid quantity and monetary values.
- Valid transaction statuses.
- Correct customer ownership.
- Correct relationships between invoices, payments and ledger entries.
- Reliable transaction boundaries for financial operations.
- Protection against duplicate submissions.

Use indexes for frequent searches, customer histories, order status, invoice lookup, payment reference lookup and date-based reporting.

Avoid loading all customers or ledger entries into the browser when server-side search and pagination are appropriate.

## 53. Historical data preservation

Preserve original order requests and financial audit history.

Do not physically delete posted invoices, posted payments or ledger history through ordinary customer or staff actions.

Implement correction and reversal behavior only according to the approved accounting policy.

Maintain sufficient historical information to explain:

- What changed.
- What the previous value was.
- What the new value is.
- Who made the change.
- When the change occurred.
- Why the change occurred when a reason is required.

